\centering
\caption{Main results (extreme heterogeneity). Metrics averaged over subgroups, $z$-levels, 100 MC runs. Bold: lowest $|\text{bias}|$. $^\dagger$RMSE exceeds Global. Moderate heterogeneity and TweetEval results---where methods converge and EC-HTE RMSE exceeds Global---in Table~\ref{tab:main_supp}; MC SEs in Appendix~\ref{app:mcse}.}
\label{tab:main_results}
\resizebox{\columnwidth}{!}{%
\begin{tabular}{ll ccc}
\toprule
Configuration & Method & $|\text{Bias}|$ & RMSE & 95\% CI Cov. \\
\midrule
\multirow{6}{*}{\shortstack[l]{DIM\\$K{=}2$, $n{=}250$}}
 & Naive & 0.058 & 0.090 & 0.82 \\
 & Global & 0.059 & 0.099 & 0.87 \\
 & F-L Scalar & 0.122 & 0.159 & 0.68 \\
 & F-L Per-sub. & 0.168 & 0.220 & 0.65 \\
 & Strat.\ MLE & 0.012 & 0.094 & 0.95 \\
 & HB EC-HTE & \textbf{0.008} & 0.089 & 0.94 \\
\midrule
\multirow{4}{*}{\shortstack[l]{DIM\\$K{=}4$, $n{=}500$}}
 & Naive & 0.062 & 0.113 & 0.88 \\
 & Global & 0.056 & 0.125 & 0.91 \\
 & Strat.\ MLE & \textbf{0.010} & 0.127$^\dagger$ & 0.95 \\
 & HB EC-HTE & 0.012 & 0.120 & 0.96 \\
\midrule
\multirow{3}{*}{\shortstack[l]{CausalForest\\$K{=}4$, $n{=}500$}}
 & Naive & 0.208 & 0.231 & 0.44 \\
 & Global & 0.191 & 0.244 & 0.58 \\
 & EC-HTE Forest & \textbf{0.039} & 0.180 & 0.90 \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}}
\end{table}

\subsection{Actionable Diagnostic Without EC-HTE}
\label{sec:diagnostic}

Proposition~\ref{prop:debiasing_hurts} provides a $\Delta\tau$-free diagnostic: global correction increases bias in subgroup $s$ iff $|\delta_s| / ((1-2\bar{\pi}) \cdot \pi_s) > 1$, computable from a pilot sample without estimating treatment effects.
A threshold classifier achieves AUC = 0.86 at the subgroup level (Figure~\ref{fig:roc}, Appendix).
On real LLM confusion matrices (16 combinations; Appendix~\ref{app:screening}), AUC drops to 0.63; restricting to subgroups with $\tau_s > 0$ recovers AUC $= 1.0$.
For $K{=}2$, $|\delta_0|{=}|\delta_1|$ limits discrimination; a meta-criterion ($\max_s \mathrm{score}(s) > 1$) recovers reliability (AUC $\geq 0.75$) for $K{\geq}4$, while $\max_s \mathrm{score}(s) \leq 1$ signals unreliable regimes (Appendix~\ref{app:screening}).

The condition number $\kappa(\hat{C}_s)$ governs correction at three levels: $\kappa > 100$ triggers the singularity guard; $\kappa \leq 1.25$ guarantees strongest bias reduction; $\kappa \in (1.25, 3]$---covering all 13 tested LLM--dataset combinations---maintains calibrated coverage ($\geq$93\% vs.\ Global 86--91\%) with RMSE gains at $n_{\text{expert}} \geq 500$ (Appendix~\ref{app:kappa_analysis}).
Budget crossover curves, $K{=}8$ scalability, and multi-LLM validation ($R^2 = 0.975$) appear in Appendices~\ref{sec:budget}--\ref{app:multilm}.

